# Batch 25 (2026-11-12): sunken cities & megalithic Europe; dragons, gods and spirits of Greece, the North, Japan, China and Ireland;
# Petra, Palmyra, Masada, Kush, southern Africa and the Americas; sky-stones, impact scars, UFO contactees, strange sounds, coincidences and hoaxes
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: sunken cities, lost lands and megalithic Europe
E+=[
X('helike','Helike: the Greek city swallowed by an earthquake and the sea (373 BCE)',H,'confirmed',2,-373,['textual','archaeological','geological/ice core'],
 "Helike was a leading city of Achaea on the Gulf of Corinth and home to a famous sanctuary of Poseidon. Ancient writers including Strabo and Pausanias report that one winter night in 373 BCE an earthquake struck and a great wave followed, and the city vanished beneath the water; for centuries visitors said its ruins could be seen below the surface. Its exact site was lost until the Helike Project, led by archaeologist Dora Katsonopoulou with Steven Soter, located its remains in 2001 buried in an ancient lagoon near the village of Rizomylos, inland rather than out at sea; excavations have since revealed layers from several periods, including an Early Bronze Age town.",
 [('A model for Atlantis?','Because Plato wrote his Atlantis story only a generation after Helike sank, and both cities were destroyed in a day and a night by earthquake and sea, some scholars think Helike may have influenced the tale. This is a suggestion, not something Plato states.'),
  ('Divine punishment','Ancient sources explained the disaster as Poseidon’s anger, after the people of Helike reportedly refused Ionian settlers a statue or model of their Poseidon sanctuary and harmed their envoys.')],
 ['wiki:Helike','url:https://www.helike.org/|The Helike Project|project site'],img='Helike'),
X('pavlopetri','Pavlopetri: the oldest known submerged town with a full street plan',H,'confirmed',3,-2800,['archaeological'],
 "Pavlopetri lies in shallow water off the coast of Laconia in the southern Peloponnese, Greece. It was spotted in 1967 by oceanographer Nicholas Flemming and mapped by a Cambridge team in 1968; a survey led by the University of Nottingham from 2009 used underwater mapping to record streets, buildings and tombs. Pottery shows occupation from the Early Bronze Age (roughly 5,000 years ago) through the Mycenaean period. The town is thought to have gone under around 1000 BCE through earthquakes and subsidence of the coast, and it is often described as the oldest submerged town with a complete plan.",
 [('Sunken-city legends','Discoveries like Pavlopetri are sometimes cited as evidence that legends of drowned cities preserve real memories; archaeologists note that each case has its own local, gradual causes rather than one shared catastrophe.')],
 ['wiki:Pavlopetri'],img='Pavlopetri',end=-1000),
X('rungholt','Rungholt: the North Sea trading town drowned in the 1362 storm flood',H,'confirmed',2,1362,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Rungholt was a trading settlement in North Frisia, on the German North Sea coast. It was destroyed by the Grote Mandrenke ('great drowning of men'), a storm surge on 15 or 16 January 1362 that killed many thousands of people along the coasts of the North Sea. Later chroniclers turned it into a moral tale: the townspeople were said to have been rich and godless, and church bells could supposedly still be heard under the waves. For a long time some doubted it was a large town at all, Ruins near Hallig Südfall were linked to Rungholt as early as 1921, and in June 2023 the German Research Foundation announced that researchers had confirmed the identification, mapping about 10 square kilometres of remains in the mudflats, including a church site.",
 [('Myth or real town?','Historians long debated whether Rungholt was a sizeable trading port or an exaggerated legend. Recent geophysical surveys support a real settlement, although how large it was is still discussed.'),
  ('Divine punishment','The legend, retold in Detlev von Liliencron’s 1882 poem ‘Trutz, Blanke Hans’, presents the flood as punishment for pride, a motif shared with Ys, Vineta and Cantre’r Gwaelod.')],
 ['wiki:Rungholt','wiki:Grote_Mandrenke'],img='Rungholt',changed="2023: survey teams reported finding the church site and settlement traces in the Wadden Sea mudflats."),
X('vineta','Vineta: the rich Baltic city said to lie beneath the sea',M,'myth-legend',3,1075,['textual','oral tradition','archaeological'],
 "Vineta is a legendary city on the southern Baltic coast, said to have been the richest city in Europe until its people's greed and pride caused it to sink beneath the sea; in some versions its bells or towers can still be glimpsed. The legend seems to grow out of medieval accounts, especially Adam of Bremen (around 1075), who described a great trading city called Jumne at the mouth of the Oder river. Many historians link this to the real Viking-age town of Wolin in Poland, where excavations have revealed a large early-medieval trading centre. Other locations, including sites near the island of Usedom and near Barth in Germany, have been proposed.",
 [('Wolin identification','The most common scholarly view is that Jumne/Vineta reflects Wolin, which flourished in the 9th–12th centuries before declining.'),
  ('A drowned city off Usedom','Local tradition and some amateur researchers place a sunken Vineta off the coast of Usedom; underwater stone formations there have been interpreted by geologists as natural boulder fields.')],
 ['wiki:Vineta','wiki:Wolin_(town)'],img='Vineta',dateNote='first described c. 1075 (Adam of Bremen)'),
X('lyonesse','Lyonesse: the lost land between Cornwall and the Isles of Scilly',M,'myth-legend',3,1150,['textual','oral tradition','geological/ice core'],
 "Lyonesse is the homeland of the hero Tristan in Arthurian romance. Later Cornish tradition placed it between Land's End and the Isles of Scilly and said it was swallowed by the sea in a single night, with only one man, Trevelyan, escaping on a white horse. Behind the legend lies a real landscape change: the Isles of Scilly were largely one island in prehistoric and possibly Roman times, and rising sea levels have since drowned the low ground between them, where submerged field walls and hut circles can still be seen at very low tides.",
 [('Memory of rising seas','Some writers see Lyonesse as a folk memory of the gradual flooding around Scilly; others think the name was simply transferred from Lothian or Léon in Brittany and attached to the Scilly landscape later.')],
 ['wiki:Lyonesse','wiki:Isles_of_Scilly'],img='Lyonesse',dateNote='Tristan romances from the 12th century; Cornish flood legend later'),
X('hyperborea','Hyperborea: the Greek land beyond the North Wind',M,'myth-legend',3,-500,['textual'],
 "In Greek mythology the Hyperboreans lived 'beyond Boreas', the North Wind, in a land of perpetual sunshine where people lived long, happy lives free of disease and war. Poets such as Pindar described it as unreachable by ship or on foot, and the god Apollo was said to spend the winter there. Herodotus, writing in the 5th century BCE, reported stories of Hyperborean offerings reaching the sanctuary of Delos but was sceptical that such people existed.",
 [('A distorted memory of the far north','Some historians suggest the stories mixed in hazy knowledge of northern peoples, the amber trade or the midnight sun; this is a possibility, not an established link.'),
  ('Esoteric and fringe uses','In the 19th and 20th centuries occultists such as Helena Blavatsky made Hyperborea an early home of humanity, and later nationalist and Nazi-era mystics linked it to Thule and an imagined northern master race. Historians regard these ideas as invented mythology with no evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Hyperborea'],img='Hyperborea',dateNote='attested from c. 6th–5th century BCE'),
X('ggantija','Ġgantija: Malta’s giant temples, older than Stonehenge',H,'confirmed',2,-3600,['archaeological','radiocarbon','oral tradition'],
 "Ġgantija is a pair of megalithic temples on the island of Gozo in Malta, built in the Neolithic between roughly 3600 and 3200 BCE, making them older than Stonehenge and the Egyptian pyramids. Their walls use huge limestone blocks, some weighing several tonnes. They are part of the UNESCO World Heritage site 'Megalithic Temples of Malta'. The name means 'giant's tower', from a local legend that a giantess built them in a single day and night while carrying a baby.",
 [('Built by giants','Maltese folklore attributes the temples to a giantess (sometimes named Sunsuna) or a race of giants. Archaeologists attribute them to Neolithic farming communities using levers and rollers; stone balls found at the sites may have served as rollers.'),
  ('Lost civilisation claims','Some alternative writers argue the Maltese temples are far older than their radiocarbon dates or link them to Atlantis. Excavated pottery and radiocarbon dates place them firmly in the 4th millennium BCE.')],
 ['wiki:Ġgantija','wiki:Megalithic_Temples_of_Malta','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/132/|UNESCO: Megalithic Temples of Malta|heritage body'],img='Ġgantija',end=-3200),
X('hal-saflieni-hypogeum','The Ħal Saflieni Hypogeum: Malta’s underground temple of the dead',H,'confirmed',3,-4000,['archaeological'],
 "The Ħal Saflieni Hypogeum in Paola, Malta, is a prehistoric underground complex of chambers cut into the rock on three levels, used between roughly 4000 and 2500 BCE. It was discovered by accident in 1902 by workers digging cisterns. The remains of an estimated 7,000 people were found there, along with figurines including the 'Sleeping Lady'. Some chambers imitate the architecture of the above-ground temples, and one, the so-called Oracle Room, is known for strong echoes.",
 [('Acoustic ritual','Some researchers have reported that the Oracle Room resonates at low frequencies and suggest the builders designed it for chanting or ritual sound. Whether the acoustic effect was intended is debated.'),
  ('Elongated skulls and lost tunnels','Online stories claim that unusual elongated skulls were found there and that a school group once vanished in its tunnels. These claims are not supported by published excavation records.')],
 ['wiki:Ħal_Saflieni_Hypogeum','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/130/|UNESCO: Ħal Saflieni Hypogeum|heritage body'],img='Ħal_Saflieni_Hypogeum',end=-2500,dateNote='in use c. 4000–2500 BCE; main Saflieni phase 3300–3000 BCE'),
X('avebury','Avebury: Britain’s largest stone circle, with a village inside it',H,'confirmed',2,-2850,['archaeological','radiocarbon','textual'],
 "Avebury in Wiltshire, England, is a Neolithic henge monument containing the largest stone circle in Britain, built and altered between roughly 2850 and 2200 BCE; a modern village stands partly inside it. In medieval times many stones were toppled and buried, perhaps because they were seen as pagan; one buried stone was found in 1938 with the skeleton of a man, thought to be a 14th-century barber-surgeon, underneath it. Antiquarian William Stukeley studied it in the 18th century, and archaeologist Alexander Keiller re-erected many stones in the 1930s. With Stonehenge it forms a UNESCO World Heritage site.",
 [('Druids and serpents','Stukeley interpreted Avebury as a Druid ‘serpent temple’; archaeology shows it predates the Druids by thousands of years.'),
  ('Earth energies','Modern dowsers and ley-line enthusiasts describe energy lines (the so-called Michael and Mary lines) passing through Avebury. These claims are not supported by scientific testing.')],
 ['wiki:Avebury','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/373/|UNESCO: Stonehenge, Avebury and Associated Sites|heritage body'],img='Avebury',end=-2200),
X('silbury-hill','Silbury Hill: Europe’s biggest prehistoric mound, purpose unknown',H,'confirmed',3,-2400,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "Silbury Hill near Avebury is the largest prehistoric artificial mound in Europe, about 30 metres high, built in several stages around 2400 BCE from chalk and earth. Its purpose remains unknown. Tunnels and shafts dug in 1776, 1849 and 1968–70 searched for a burial but found none; the 1968 tunnel was dug for a BBC-televised excavation, and later repairs in 2007–08 recovered material that helped refine the date.",
 [('Buried king legend','Local legend says a King Sil, or Zel, is buried inside on a golden horse; no burial has ever been found.'),
  ('Astronomical or symbolic','Proposals range from a symbol of the land or a harvest monument to an astronomical marker or a platform for ritual; none has been confirmed.')],
 ['wiki:Silbury_Hill','url:https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/silbury-hill/|English Heritage: Silbury Hill|heritage body'],img='Silbury_Hill'),
]
# --- part 2: gods, dragons and spirits
E+=[
X('prometheus','Prometheus: the Titan who stole fire for humanity',M,'myth-legend',2,-700,['textual'],
 "In Greek myth the Titan Prometheus tricks Zeus and steals fire from the gods to give to humans. As punishment Zeus has him chained to a rock, traditionally in the Caucasus, where an eagle eats his liver every day and it grows back every night, until the hero Heracles frees him. The earliest full accounts are in Hesiod's Theogony and Works and Days (around 700 BCE), and the play Prometheus Bound, traditionally attributed to Aeschylus, made him a symbol of defiance. In Hesiod, Zeus answers the theft of fire by creating Pandora, the first woman.",
 [('Fire-bringer myths worldwide','Stories of a trickster or hero stealing fire for people appear in many cultures, from the Georgian hero Amirani (also chained in the Caucasus) to Maui in Polynesia and animal fire-thieves in Native American traditions. Scholars debate whether the Greek and Georgian stories share a common source or influenced each other.'),
  ('The regrowing liver','Some modern writers note that the liver is the one human organ able to regenerate and wonder whether the Greeks knew this; others reply that the detail is a natural choice for endless punishment and need not reflect medical knowledge.')],
 ['wiki:Prometheus','wiki:Prometheus_Bound'],img='Prometheus',dateNote='Hesiod, c. 700 BCE'),
X('medusa','Medusa and the Gorgons: the face that turns people to stone',M,'myth-legend',2,-700,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Medusa was one of three Gorgon sisters in Greek myth, with snakes for hair and a gaze that turned anyone who looked at her to stone. The hero Perseus killed her by looking only at her reflection in a polished shield, and from her neck sprang the winged horse Pegasus. In the version told by the Roman poet Ovid, she was once a beautiful woman punished by Athena after Poseidon assaulted her in Athena's temple. Images of a Gorgon's face, the gorgoneion, were placed on temples, shields and armour from the Archaic period onwards as protection against evil.",
 [('A protective symbol first?','Some scholars think the frightening Gorgon face began as an apotropaic (evil-averting) mask or image, and the story of Medusa grew around it.'),
  ('Modern readings','In recent decades Medusa has been reinterpreted as a symbol of female rage and of victims blamed for the harm done to them, drawing on Ovid’s version.')],
 ['wiki:Medusa','wiki:Gorgoneion'],img='Medusa',dateNote='in Greek art and poetry from c. 700 BCE'),
X('beowulf','Beowulf: the Old English hero who fought Grendel and a dragon',M,'myth-legend',2,1000,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Beowulf is an epic poem in Old English, surviving in a single manuscript (the Nowell Codex) copied around 975–1025; when the poem itself was composed is disputed, with suggestions ranging from the 8th to the early 11th century. It tells of the Geatish hero Beowulf, who kills the monster Grendel and Grendel's mother at the Danish king's hall Heorot, and decades later dies fighting a treasure-guarding dragon. The manuscript was damaged in the 1731 fire at the Cotton library. Some characters, like the Geatish king Hygelac, match a raid on Frankish lands recorded by Gregory of Tours in the early 6th century.",
 [('Real halls and kings','Excavations at Lejre in Denmark revealed large royal halls of the right period, and some scholars link them with Heorot; the ship burial at Sutton Hoo is often compared with the poem’s funeral scenes. The monsters remain fiction.'),
  ('Grendel as a real creature','Some young-Earth creationist writers have claimed Grendel was a surviving dinosaur. Scholars see Grendel as a literary monster descended from Cain, as the poem itself says.')],
 ['wiki:Beowulf','wiki:Lejre'],img='Beowulf',dateNote='manuscript c. 975–1025; composition date debated'),
X('sigurd-fafnir','Sigurd and the dragon Fafnir',M,'myth-legend',2,1030,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Sigurd (Siegfried in German) is the greatest dragon-slayer of Norse and Germanic legend. In the Poetic Edda and the 13th-century Völsunga saga, he kills the dragon Fafnir, once a dwarf turned into a dragon by greed for a cursed gold hoard, by hiding in a pit and stabbing it from below with the reforged sword Gram. Tasting the dragon's heart-blood, he understands the speech of birds. The story was carved on the Ramsund rock in Sweden around 1030, and a related German version forms the Nibelungenlied, later reworked by Richard Wagner in his Ring cycle.",
 [('History inside the legend','The Nibelung stories include figures based on real 5th-century people, such as the Burgundian king Gundahar (Gunnar/Gunther), whose kingdom was destroyed by Hunnic forces in 437, and Attila (Atli/Etzel).'),
  ('Dragons from fossils?','Some writers suggest that finds of large fossil bones fed European dragon legends, as possibly happened elsewhere. For Fafnir specifically there is no direct evidence of this.')],
 ['wiki:Sigurd','wiki:Fafnir','wiki:Ramsund_carving'],img='Ramsund_carving',dateNote='Ramsund carving c. 1030; sagas written 13th century'),
X('amaterasu-cave','Amaterasu hides in the cave: the day the sun went out',M,'myth-legend',2,712,['textual'],
 "In Japanese myth, recorded in the Kojiki (712) and Nihon Shoki (720), the sun goddess Amaterasu is so upset by the violent behaviour of her brother, the storm god Susanoo, that she shuts herself inside a rock cave, Ama-no-Iwato, plunging the world into darkness. The other gods gather outside, and the goddess Ame-no-Uzume performs a wild dance that makes them roar with laughter. Curious, Amaterasu peeks out, sees her reflection in a mirror hung on a tree and is drawn out, and light returns. The mirror is identified with the Yata no Kagami, one of the three Imperial Regalia of Japan.",
 [('Memory of an eclipse','Some writers have suggested the story preserves the memory of a solar eclipse; others link it to winter-solstice rituals to call back the sun. Neither link can be proved.'),
  ('Ritual origins','Scholars note that the dance is treated as the origin of kagura, Shinto sacred dance, and that the myth supports the imperial family’s claimed descent from Amaterasu.')],
 ['wiki:Amaterasu','wiki:Ama-no-Iwato'],img='Amaterasu'),
X('yamata-no-orochi','Yamata no Orochi: the eight-headed serpent and the sword in its tail',M,'myth-legend',3,712,['textual'],
 "After being expelled from heaven, the storm god Susanoo arrives in Izumo, western Japan, and meets an elderly couple whose daughters have been eaten one by one by Yamata no Orochi, a huge serpent with eight heads and eight tails. Susanoo has eight vats of strong sake set out; the serpent drinks itself into a stupor and he cuts it to pieces. In one tail he finds the sword Kusanagi, later one of the Imperial Regalia. The story appears in the Kojiki (712) and Nihon Shoki (720).",
 [('A river in flood','A common interpretation sees the many-headed serpent as the Hii River and its tributaries in flood, tamed by human work.'),
  ('Iron-working','Others note that the Izumo region was a centre of iron production from iron sand, which turned rivers red, and read the sword in the tail as a symbol of iron-working. These readings are interpretations, not proven.')],
 ['wiki:Yamata_no_Orochi'],img='Yamata_no_Orochi'),
X('sun-wukong','Sun Wukong and Journey to the West: a real pilgrimage turned into myth',M,'myth-legend',2,1592,['textual'],
 "Journey to the West is a Chinese novel published in 1592 and traditionally attributed to Wu Cheng'en. It turns the real 7th-century journey of the monk Xuanzang to India into a fantasy: the monk is protected by the Monkey King Sun Wukong, born from a stone, who has defied heaven and been imprisoned under a mountain by the Buddha, along with the pig-man Zhu Bajie and the river ogre Sha Wujing. It is one of the Four Great Classical Novels of Chinese literature and has inspired countless operas, films, cartoons and games.",
 [('Hanuman connection','In the 1920s the scholar Hu Shih proposed that Sun Wukong was influenced by Hanuman, the monkey hero of the Ramayana; Lu Xun argued instead for a native Chinese origin in the water monster Wuzhiqi. Both views still have supporters.'),
  ('Real journey behind the story','Xuanzang’s own account, Great Tang Records on the Western Regions (646), is a valuable historical source on Central and South Asia and is far more down-to-earth than the novel.')],
 ['wiki:Journey_to_the_West','wiki:Sun_Wukong'],img='Sun_Wukong'),
X('jiangshi','Jiangshi: China’s hopping corpses',M,'myth-legend',3,1750,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "The jiangshi ('stiff corpse') is a reanimated corpse in Chinese folklore that moves by hopping with its arms stretched out, because rigor mortis keeps its body stiff, and drains the life force (qi) of the living. Stories of such corpses appear in Qing dynasty collections of strange tales, such as those by Ji Yun and Yuan Mei in the 18th century. Folk explanations connect the image to the practice of 'transporting corpses over a thousand li' in western Hunan, in which the bodies of people who died far from home were said to be carried back, sometimes at night, by Taoist priests. Hong Kong films such as Mr. Vampire (1985) made the jiangshi famous worldwide.",
 [('Corpse-walkers explained','One explanation for hopping corpses is that bodies were tied to bamboo poles carried on the shoulders of two men, making them appear to bounce in the dark. This is a popular explanation and is hard to verify historically.'),
  ('Vampire parallels','The jiangshi is often called the ‘Chinese vampire’, and it shares with European revenant panics the fear that improper burial lets the dead return to harm the living.')],
 ['wiki:Jiangshi'],img='Jiangshi',dateNote='Qing dynasty tales, 18th century'),
X('banshee','The banshee: the Irish fairy woman who wails before a death',M,'myth-legend',3,1600,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "In Irish folklore the banshee (bean sí, 'woman of the fairy mound') is a female spirit whose keening or wailing at night foretells a death in certain families, traditionally old Irish families. She is described in different ways: as a young woman, a washerwoman cleaning bloodstained clothes at a ford, or an old woman combing her long hair. The figure is closely linked to the real tradition of keening, the lament sung by women at Irish wakes and funerals.",
 [('Sounds in the night','Sceptics suggest that some reported wails were the screeches of barn owls or foxes, given meaning after a death. Believers treat the banshee as a real family omen.'),
  ('Older goddess roots','Some folklorists connect the banshee to earlier Irish figures such as the war goddess the Morrígan and the washer at the ford who foretells warriors’ deaths in medieval tales.')],
 ['wiki:Banshee','wiki:Keening'],img='Banshee',dateNote='folk belief recorded over many centuries; placed here approximately'),
X('changelings','Changelings: fairy babies swapped for human children',M,'myth-legend',3,1500,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "Across European folklore, especially in Ireland, Britain and Scandinavia, fairies or trolls were believed to steal human babies and leave one of their own, or an enchanted log, in their place. A 'changeling' might be sickly, cry constantly or fail to grow. Martin Luther recounted such a case in his Table Talk in the 16th century, and the belief had tragic consequences as late as 1895, when Bridget Cleary was killed in County Tipperary, Ireland, by her husband, who claimed she had been replaced by a fairy.",
 [('Explaining illness and disability','Historians and medical writers suggest the belief helped people explain children with illnesses or developmental conditions that were not understood at the time, sometimes with harmful results for those children.'),
  ('Fairy abduction tradition','Changeling lore belongs to a wider body of stories in which fairies take people into their world, linked to the Irish sídhe and Scandinavian huldufolk traditions.')],
 ['wiki:Changeling','wiki:Bridget_Cleary'],img='Changeling',dateNote='widespread from the Middle Ages to the 19th century'),
]
